{"kind":"assert","text":"NF-4 (d2aff067) needs four rulings before any code. The source work sits on NF-3 head 3bb7327c, and nothing is edited yet. `#installApplication` (service.ts:1432-1512) uses four private McpService parts that the request does not place. I propose one answer for each.\n\nQ1 Reads. The install and installation reads go through MCP's `Reads` (packages/mcp/src/reads.ts, 157 lines), with read sessions and a signed-read fallback. The command line cannot import it. (A) Move `Reads` to the client, and have the client functions take a reader port with the signature of `Reads.read(membership, run)`. (B) Give the command line a smaller reader built on the client's `requestSession` and `signedReads`. I propose A: a larger head, but no second reader.\n\nQ2 Admission. `#admitTarget` applies MCP's tool policy before signing: the tool must be selected, and the member's standing must allow the grant. I propose an optional `admit` hook on the client function. MCP passes its check, the command line passes none, and the scope judges the install. That matches what the scope does today for any client.\n\nQ3 Sending. `#sendAct` keeps a person's originals at membership and dispatches hosted calls. I propose a `send` port with the signature of `custody.send`. MCP passes `#sendAct`, and the command line passes plain `custody.send`, as its path does today.\n\nQ4 Wording. MCP builds every refusal with `failed({error, ...})` through errors.ts and words.ts. I propose that the client functions return typed refusals such as `{ refused: \"target\", what }`, and that MCP maps them back to `failed(...)`, with tests that fix the MCP wording byte for byte. The command line gets short wording of its own, so its error lines change. The alternative is to move the error words to the client too. The request says nothing about command-line wording.\n\n`#invalidate` stays in MCP after the call. The client functions return typed refusals and do not throw `Stop`."}